  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nNorthwesterly upper flow is forecast to be overhead Saturday \nmorning. Ensemble guidance indicates the presence of a shortwave \ntrough embedded in the flow that will traverse the forecast region \nSaturday. However, it is unclear how strong this system will be once \nit enters the area. When looking at LREF contributors for the 25 \nNovember 00Z run time, around 50-60% of GEFS and ENS members show a \nmodestly deep trough and associated jet streak along the trailing \nedge that could be upwards of 70 kts. On the other hand, the \nremaining ENS and GEFS members, along with most of the GEPS members \nshow this wave as a shallow disturbance. The solution with a deeper \nshortwave would express itself as a post-frontal mixed precipitation \nevent Saturday as a strong surface low pulls a moist airmass \nsouthward into the area. NBM guidance shows temperatures falling \nbelow freezing sometime between the mid evening and nighttime hours, \nwhich is when wintry precipitation would become more likely. When \nlooking at the LREF Grand Ensemble for 24 hr snowfall, much of the \narea has a 20-40% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of snowfall \nSaturday, with the highest of these probabilities in Western Yuma \nand Kit Carson Counties in Colorado. Even so, about 25% of the LREF \nensemble members show the shallow disturbance, which correspond only \nto a 40% chance or less for any precipitation occurring at all. The \nfavored solution at this time is the deeper shortwave with mixed \nprecipitation, at around 40-50%. Still, confidence in a Winter \nWeather Advisory being needed is low (10-15%), as even the 95th \npercentile from the LREF Grand Ensemble shows 2 inches of \naccumulation or less. \n\nEnsembles show northwesterly flow remaining overhead Sunday, with \nthe embedded shortwave trough exiting to the east. As this occurs, \nsurface flow is favored to be slowing a bit, but remain out of the \nnorth to northwest for the majority of the morning and afternoon. \nNBM guidance suggests a 75% chance or greater for most areas in the \nforecast region to be at or below the lower 50s for high \ntemperatures. This estimate can vary a bit though depending on how \nSaturday's event plays out. If colder temperatures and snowfall can \noccur Saturday, the lower end would likely be experienced. However, \ntemperatures in the 50s could be supported if the warmer, drier \nsolution takes place. Looking at 24 hr precipitation guidance, dry \nconditions are expected throughout the day Sunday. \n\nBy Monday morning, upper flow is forecast to shift to zonal (west to \neast) and remain through the end of the forecast period. Model \nguidance shows some shortwave troughs embedded in this flow that \ncould impact the region Monday and Wednesday, though PoPs \n(probabilities for precipitation) are below 10% through Wednesday \nafternoon. There is a portion of LREF solutions (15% total) that \nshow a 20-40% chance of precipitation occurring Monday in \nassociation with a deeper shortwave, but dry conditions are favored \notherwise. Current NBM guidance supports high temperatures in the \nupper 40s or higher during this period, with some higher end \nscenarios in the 60s Tuesday and Wednesday.",
